-\section{Fast Detection with Representative Observations}
+\section{Fast Detection from RDMA Delivery Observations}
 \label{sec:detection}
-\subsection{Observation Contract}
+\subsection{Inputs and Healthy Calibration}
+The native detector, \texttt{canary-service-v4}, monitors 32 original paths:
+16 senders, each with an A and a B path to its ring successor. It receives
+actual QP submission/completion events, sender retransmissions, business
+ACK prefixes and receiver-local accepted-packet times. Fault onset, affected
+node, injected service fraction, drop probability and future recovery time
+are available only to the injector and offline scorer.
+
+Each path sends a real 64-KiB RDMA canary every 2~ms, starting at 1~ms and
+continuing to the observation horizon. The first 20~ms freezes the maximum
+completed canary service time $b$; this interval is assumed healthy.
+The deadline is $\max(100~\mu\mathrm{s},2b+100~\mu\mathrm{s})$, with a
+2-ms fallback when no completed calibration sample exists. The fallback
+does not establish coverage of arbitrary startup degradation.
+
+\subsection{Three Complementary Evidence Lanes}
+\paragraph{Repeated canary impairment.}
+A canary becomes bad when it exceeds its deadline or actually retransmits.
+An outstanding canary can count before completion. Repeated retransmissions
+of the same canary count once. At least three distinct bad canaries in a
+trailing 40-ms window, with submission times spanning at least 4~ms, trigger
+an alarm. The 2-ms cadence therefore supplies repeated evidence well before
+100~ms when the impairment affects canary service.
+
+\paragraph{Business progress stall.}
+An allocated, unfinished original-path chunk with no real ACK-prefix
+progress for 40~ms supplies a second alarm lane. Idle work, unallocated
+work and already migrated epochs do not supply this signal. This lane
+can expose delivery stalls that surviving canaries fail to reveal.
+
+\paragraph{Receiver spacing shift.}
+Completion-time thresholds alone missed capacity reductions in the first
+formal validation. v4 adds the P10 of positive interarrival gaps between
+adjacent accepted packets on the same original path, aggregating business
+and canary QPs. Both packets must have a full 976-byte payload; a short tail
+breaks adjacency. The 1--21-ms interval calibrates the P10. Thereafter,
+two consecutive nonoverlapping 10-ms windows, each with at least 64 gaps,
+must exceed $2\times$ the baseline P10 plus 100~ns. Insufficient samples
+or a healthy window interrupt confirmation. This is a model-specific
+service-change rule, not a general capacity estimator or a way to distinguish
+all congestion from faults.
+
+\subsection{Alarm Delivery and Measurement Assumptions}
+The first qualifying lane emits one alarm per path. A 96-byte alarm pays
+serialization on a shared 100-Mb/s management FIFO plus 1-ms propagation.
+Latency is scored at actual controller receipt, including queueing, rather
+than at the earlier detector callback. Fast evidence accumulation, not
+post hoc subtraction of control time, explains the measured sub-100-ms result.
+
+Endpoint timestamps and local summaries are ideal simulator observations.
+The implementation feeds endpoint evidence into a shared detector object;
+it does not separately serialize every raw observation or spacing summary
+between hosts. Clock error, timestamp noise and callback CPU time are not
+modeled. Thus charging alarm transport is not evidence that a distributed
+telemetry implementation has been validated. The older explicitly reported
+UDP observer has a different observation contract and remains in
+Appendix~\ref{app:historical-udp}.
+
+\section{Cost-Aware Replacement and Coordinated Execution}
+\label{sec:recovery-design}
+\subsection{Measure the Candidate Before Choosing It}
+The candidate route uses three store-and-forward legs: source GPU to a local
+helper, helper to remote helper over healthy B accesses, and remote helper
+to the original destination. Candidate eligibility uses modeled phase-idle
+state and resource credits. It is not an implemented distributed discovery
+protocol. A full 64-KiB probe measures all three legs; its 96-byte health
+report must actually arrive through the control FIFO before use. Freshness
+is measured from physical probe completion, including report transit, with
+a 20-ms limit. Both compared replacement policies pay the same candidate
+measurement and health-report costs.
+
+\subsection{Compare Finite Remaining Work}
+For each sender/phase, the controller reconstructs A-owned and B-owned
+unacknowledged bytes plus flexible pending bytes. Direct service is the ACK
+progress observed over the previous 10~ms. If a lane has no owned bytes and
+no recent business progress, a fresh completed canary can supply a service
+estimate. Observed zero service is not knowledge that the fault will persist.
+
+Let $F(a,b,p;r_A,r_B,d)$ be the fluid finish estimate: A serves immediately,
+B serves after delay $d$, owned bytes $a,b$ remain assigned to their lanes,
+and pending bytes $p$ can use spare service on either lane. The implementation
+finds the earliest feasible completion time by bisection. With probe leg
+times $\ell_1,\ell_2,\ell_3$, final-notification estimate $q$ and chunk size
+$C=64$~KiB, the candidate service estimate is
+\[
+\widehat r_H=\min\left\{\frac{C}{\max_j\ell_j},
+\frac{4C}{\ell_1+\ell_2+\ell_3+q}\right\}.
+\]
+The four-credit limit bounds overlap; it does not divide the first chunk's
+end-to-end latency by four. The switch estimate charges pipeline fill,
+current management queueing, four-party coordination and final notification.
+Even a short rescued tail pays a complete first route/ACK cycle.
+
+The policy compares $F$ under observed direct service with $F$ under the
+measured helper service and its setup delay. It switches only when estimated
+gain exceeds $\max(1~\mathrm{ms},0.1\widehat T_{\rm stay})$. A finite helper
+estimate can justify replacing an observed zero-service owned lane.
+The gate is checked after the paid probe and again before initial commit;
+rejected attempts wait 5~ms before retrying. This is a sender/phase heuristic,
+not a global DAG optimizer. Access to modeled helper eligibility and current
+control-queue state is an explicit simulation assumption.
+
+\subsection{Logical Ownership and Completion}
+PREPARE, READY and COMMIT coordinate the source, destination and two helpers.
+Messages pay the same 96-byte, 100-Mb/s, 1-ms-propagation FIFO costs as alarms
+and health reports. Phase and binding version reject stale reports; helper
+idleness and freshness are rechecked before activation. Replacement keeps
+the physical A/B topology, retires the old B allocation and rescues the
+unacknowledged suffix under a new chunk epoch. Old-epoch ACKs cannot advance
+that logical suffix. Three completed legs and a final source notification
+are required before logical completion.
+
+The audit checks physical NIC selection, helper and source credits, ACK
+prefix monotonicity, route epochs and final delivery accounting. These are
+logical communication invariants, not tensor reduction correctness. A proxy
+activation or rescued suffix also does not imply sustained use of B$'$ for
+new chunks; that outcome is measured separately.
+
+\section{Evaluation}
+\subsection{Registered Model, Metrics and Internal Controls}
+The new packet experiments use the ns-3/RDMA backend in the collaborator's
+standalone simulator, run on CPUs without real GPUs. The topology has 16
+logical GPUs, four per server, physical A/B planes and 10-Gb/s accesses.
+Normal local helper links are modeled at 2400~Gb/s; the 1-Gb/s helper is an
+artificial stress boundary, not a hardware configuration claim.
+
+The ordinary workload has two ring rounds, 60 phases and 64 chunks of
+64~KiB per rank/phase, totaling 4,026,531,840 logical bytes. Work starts at
+1~ms and observation ends at 400~ms. The slow-helper plus quarter-capacity
+boundary deliberately uses one round, 30 phases and 256 chunks per
+rank/phase, totaling 8,053,063,680 bytes, with an 800-ms horizon. All arms
+within a pair share workload and horizon; cross-row speedup comparisons
+between these workloads are invalid.
+
+We design the fault injections; they are not drawn from a datacenter trace.
+A chosen GPU's B access receives a blackhole, service fraction 0.25,
+independent per-packet loss 0.005, or 80-ms channel propagation delay.
+Placement is drawn from 16 GPUs and onset from 35--55~ms. Both directions
+of the access are impaired. Extra boundaries use a 2-ms blackhole, 1-Gb/s
+helper links, or B blackholes on all four GPUs in server 0. The last case
+removes idle local helper candidates.
+
+The main matrix contains 580 registered attempts: four benefit-policy fault
+cohorts of 60, 60 healthy controls, 20 three-arm pairs per main fault type
+and 10 three-arm pairs per extra boundary. The main cohorts already include
+the benefit arm of each pair. A separate 60-run weak-loss confirmation
+brings the new total to 640. Native source, binary, parameters and inputs
+are frozen before execution; development cases are not acceptance samples.
+
+The three internal arms share adaptive A/B allocation and detection:
+\emph{no proxy} (G10) never replaces B, \emph{direct replacement} (G11)
+coordinates a qualified candidate after alarm and probe, and \emph{benefit
+gate} (G11) adds the cost comparison. ``Direct'' does not mean zero-cost
+or immediate migration. These are mechanism controls, not external-system
+baselines. Ring versus reconstructed OptCC remains a separate scheduling
+comparison (Section~\ref{sec:baseline-reproduction}); VCCL, SprayCheck and
+Chameleon remain literature comparisons.
+
