+of a published performance figure. It omits native collective memory and
+reduction costs. Appendix~\ref{app:optcc} preserves artifact, transport,
+scheduler and segmentation checks. The result establishes an executable
+schedule comparator, not LIMER superiority. The planned common-backend
+matrix crosses ring/OptCC schedules with stay/fixed-switch/LIMER route
+policies under identical faults, workload and seeds, charging all recovery
+cost and retaining failures. That joint comparison remains unrun. The new 16-GPU helper-recovery
+backend and the earlier reconstructed scheduling runner are not a matched
+full-system comparison.
+
+\section{Limitations and Remaining Validation}
+\label{sec:limitations}
+The detector and helper recovery are now evaluated together in a native
+packet-level simulation. This closes the earlier separation between isolated
+detection and route components, but only under the newly registered model.
+It does not establish general fast gray-fault coverage. The pass depends on
+a healthy 20-ms startup reference, fixed canary geometry, ideal nanosecond
+endpoint timestamps and centrally available local summaries. Raw observation
+exchange, timestamp noise and detector processing time are not separately
+charged. Only alarms and the declared recovery/control traffic pay the
+management FIFO. Arbitrary background traffic, startup degradation,
+correlated faults and multiple episodes remain unvalidated.
+
+The healthy control repeats one deterministic trajectory. The selected
+weak-loss detection cohort fixes the derived-stream reuse issue, while
+main policy pairs remain empirical comparisons under the original stream
+schedule. The confidence bound is per condition under sampling assumptions,
+not a guarantee over all fault distributions. Earlier failed validations
+remain failures.
+
+Benefit gating approximates sender/phase completion from recent service and
+candidate probes. It assumes access to modeled helper eligibility and
+control-queue state; distributed discovery and uncertainty-aware congestion
+prediction are not implemented. The policy loses time under persistent
+blackhole and weak loss, and the favorable slow-helper case uses a deliberately
+extreme local-link boundary. No idle helper means detection cannot produce
+recovery. Sustained new B$'$ throughput, candidate secondary failure and
+restoration of old B have not been demonstrated.
+
+Execution checks establish logical epochs, prefixes and final-delivery
+accounting. The simulator does not execute tensor reduction, CUDA, HBM or
+compute/communication overlap. There is no completed full LIMER versus
+OptCC/VCCL comparison or 128-rank validation. The earlier UDP and pending-only
+experiments retain separate time bases and are not combined with the new
+loop into additional end-to-end evidence. Real-GPU execution and deployment
+are outside the agreed simulation scope.
+
+\section{Conclusion}
+LIMER now implements a connected RDMA simulation loop for timely impairment
+detection, measured helper-route benefit decisions and coordinated rescue.
+Four selected registered detection cohorts each achieve 60/60 correctly
+localized controller alarms below 100~ms, with a worst per-condition p95 of
+43.12~ms. Matched policy experiments show both the benefit of avoiding an
+expensive helper route and the latency cost of waiting under persistent
+faults. Full denominators, independent replay, the failed first validation
+and explicit measurement assumptions bound these findings. General gray-fault
+coverage, sustained replacement-plane throughput and external full-system
+comparisons remain open simulation work.
+
